<h2>The bond is a contract with the court</h2>
<p>A bail bond binds the accused — and, through the bail bond, the sureties — to the condition that he will appear when required. The surety&rsquo;s promise is not ornamental: it is a monetary undertaking that the law enforces when the condition fails. The BNSS also softens the old regime in one respect: courts may accept a personal bond without sureties in appropriate cases, and may permit an indigent person to be released on such a bond, but where sureties are taken, their liability is real.</p>

<h2>How forfeiture proceedings unfold</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Breach</strong> — the accused fails to appear; the court issues process (bailable or non-bailable warrants) and records that the bond condition stands breached.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Proof and notice</strong> — under Section 491 BNSS the court records the grounds of its satisfaction and issues notice to the accused and the sureties to pay the penalty or show cause against it.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Show cause</strong> — the surety explains: illness or death of the accused, circumstances beyond control, efforts made to produce him, or that the breach itself is disputed.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Order</strong> — if cause is insufficient, the court may order recovery of the penalty, in whole or — in its discretion — in part, remitting the balance; recovery proceeds as if the amount were a fine.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Appeal</strong> — the penalty order is appealable to the next higher court; the appellate court examines both the finding of breach and the refusal to remit.</div>
</div>

<h2>What saves a surety</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Produce the accused</h3><p>Nothing mitigates like production. A surety who traces and produces the absconder, even late, has the strongest claim to substantial remission.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Show diligence</h3><p>Evidence of actual effort — visits to the accused&rsquo;s address, police complaints, communication records — distinguishes the deceived surety from the indifferent one.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Attack the procedure</h3><p>Forfeiture without recorded grounds, without notice, or without a real opportunity to show cause is vulnerable in appeal. The structure of Section 491 is mandatory, not decorative.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Timely discharge</h3><p>A surety may apply to be discharged from the bond before any breach; the court then secures the accused afresh. Hesitation is what converts anxiety into liability.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Connected consequences for the accused</h2>
<p>Forfeiture rarely travels alone. The absconding accused faces cancellation of bail, non-bailable warrants, proclamation proceedings, and a separate offence of failure to appear. When he resurfaces, the earlier forfeiture becomes a weighty factor against fresh bail — courts ask who will stand surety for a man whose last sureties paid for his absence, and insist on stricter conditions, higher amounts, or both.</p>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>Before signing as surety, a person should know:</strong> the bond amount is a genuine personal exposure; the obligation continues until the case ends or the court discharges the bond; a change in the accused&rsquo;s address or conduct should prompt an immediate discharge application; and signing on the basis of forged documents or for consideration is itself an offence.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>For families deciding whether to stand surety, the honest advice is this: sign only for a person whose appearance you can realistically influence, keep in contact throughout the trial, and treat the first missed date as the emergency it is. The law gives a diligent surety several exits; it gives a passive one a recovery notice.</p>
</div>
